{"subjectId":"dsa-basics","id":"stack","title":"Stack","group":"Stacks & Queues","order":14,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A stack is a <strong>Last In, First Out</strong> (LIFO) collection: you can only add to or remove from one end, the <em>top</em>. It is the data structure behind function calls, undo, bracket matching and expression evaluation.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Stack</strong> — a linear data structure where insertion (<code>push</code>) and deletion (<code>pop</code>) happen at the same end, called the top. The element pushed last is popped first.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>A stack after push(10), push(20), push(30), push(40)</figcaption><pre><code>push(40)  →   ┌────┐  ← top        pop()  →  returns 40\n              │ 40 │\n              ├────┤\n              │ 30 │\n              ├────┤\n              │ 20 │\n              ├────┤\n              │ 10 │  ← bottom\n              └────┘</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>Operations</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>What it does</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>push(x)</code></td><td>Insert <code>x</code> at the top</td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td><code>pop()</code></td><td>Remove and return the top element</td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td><code>peek()</code> / <code>top()</code></td><td>Return the top element without removing it</td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td><code>isEmpty()</code></td><td><code>true</code> when the stack has no elements</td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td><code>size()</code></td><td>Number of elements</td><td>O(1)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><strong>Underflow</strong> — calling <code>pop()</code> or <code>peek()</code> on an empty stack. <strong>Overflow</strong> — calling <code>push()</code> on a fixed-capacity stack that is full. Always check <code>isEmpty()</code> before popping; the built-in containers below raise an error (Python/Java) or return <code>undefined</code> (JavaScript) instead.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Using the built-in containers</h3>\n<p>Every language already has a container that works as a stack. Use the end of a dynamic array (append / pop at the back are amortised O(1)) — never the front, where every operation shifts all elements.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>stack = []                 # a list used as a stack\nstack.append('a')          # push\nstack.append('b')\nstack.append('c')\nprint(\"top:\", stack[-1])   # peek\nprint(\"popped:\", stack.pop())\nprint(\"popped:\", stack.pop())\nprint(\"size:\", len(stack))\nprint(\"empty:\", len(stack) == 0)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stack&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    stack&lt;char&gt; st;            // std::stack adapts a deque\n    st.push('a');\n    st.push('b');\n    st.push('c');\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"top: \" &lt;&lt; st.top() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"popped: \" &lt;&lt; st.top() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\"; st.pop();   // pop() returns nothing in C++\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"popped: \" &lt;&lt; st.top() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\"; st.pop();\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"size: \" &lt;&lt; st.size() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"empty: \" &lt;&lt; (st.empty() ? \"True\" : \"False\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Deque&lt;Character&gt; stack = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();   // preferred over the legacy java.util.Stack\n        stack.push('a');\n        stack.push('b');\n        stack.push('c');\n        System.out.println(\"top: \" + stack.peek());\n        System.out.println(\"popped: \" + stack.pop());\n        System.out.println(\"popped: \" + stack.pop());\n        System.out.println(\"size: \" + stack.size());\n        System.out.println(\"empty: \" + (stack.isEmpty() ? \"True\" : \"False\"));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const stack = [];              // an array used as a stack\nstack.push('a');\nstack.push('b');\nstack.push('c');\nconsole.log(\"top:\", stack[stack.length - 1]);\nconsole.log(\"popped:\", stack.pop());\nconsole.log(\"popped:\", stack.pop());\nconsole.log(\"size:\", stack.length);\nconsole.log(\"empty:\", stack.length === 0 ? \"True\" : \"False\");</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>top: c\npopped: c\npopped: b\nsize: 1\nempty: False</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Python's <code>collections.deque</code> and <code>queue.LifoQueue</code> also work as stacks. <code>deque</code> is the right choice when the same container must also behave as a queue; <code>LifoQueue</code> is thread-safe and meant for producer/consumer code, not for algorithms.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i>Implementing a stack with a linked list</h3>\n<p>Pushing and popping at the head of a singly linked list are both O(1), so a linked list gives a stack that never needs resizing. The head node is the top.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>push(10), push(20), push(30) — the newest node becomes the head</figcaption><pre><code>top\n ↓\n┌────┬───┐   ┌────┬───┐   ┌────┬───┐\n│ 30 │ ●─┼──▶│ 20 │ ●─┼──▶│ 10 │ ∅ │\n└────┴───┘   └────┴───┘   └────┴───┘</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.next = None\n\nclass Stack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.head = None\n        self.size = 0\n\n    def push(self, value):\n        node = Node(value)\n        node.next = self.head      # new node points at the old top\n        self.head = node\n        self.size += 1\n\n    def pop(self):\n        if self.head is None:\n            raise IndexError(\"pop from empty stack\")\n        node = self.head\n        self.head = node.next\n        self.size -= 1\n        return node.value\n\n    def peek(self):\n        return None if self.head is None else self.head.value\n\n    def is_empty(self):\n        return self.head is None\n\ns = Stack()\nfor x in (10, 20, 30):\n    s.push(x)\nnode = s.head\nwhile node:                      # print top → bottom\n    print(node.value, end=\" \")\n    node = node.next\nprint()\nprint(\"size:\", s.size)\nprint(\"popped:\", s.pop())\nprint(\"peek:\", s.peek())</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int value;\n    Node* next;\n    Node(int v) : value(v), next(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nclass Stack {\n    Node* head = nullptr;\n    int count = 0;\npublic:\n    ~Stack() { while (head) pop(); }\n    void push(int v) {\n        Node* node = new Node(v);\n        node-&gt;next = head;           // new node points at the old top\n        head = node;\n        count++;\n    }\n    int pop() {\n        if (!head) throw runtime_error(\"pop from empty stack\");\n        Node* node = head;\n        int v = node-&gt;value;\n        head = node-&gt;next;\n        delete node;\n        count--;\n        return v;\n    }\n    int peek() const { return head-&gt;value; }\n    bool isEmpty() const { return head == nullptr; }\n    int size() const { return count; }\n    Node* top() const { return head; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Stack s;\n    for (int x : {10, 20, 30}) s.push(x);\n    for (Node* n = s.top(); n; n = n-&gt;next) cout &lt;&lt; n-&gt;value &lt;&lt; \" \";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"size: \" &lt;&lt; s.size() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"popped: \" &lt;&lt; s.pop() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"peek: \" &lt;&lt; s.peek() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        int value; Node next;\n        Node(int v) { value = v; }\n    }\n\n    static class Stack {\n        Node head; int size;\n        void push(int v) {\n            Node node = new Node(v);\n            node.next = head;            // new node points at the old top\n            head = node;\n            size++;\n        }\n        int pop() {\n            if (head == null) throw new RuntimeException(\"pop from empty stack\");\n            int v = head.value;\n            head = head.next;\n            size--;\n            return v;\n        }\n        Integer peek() { return head == null ? null : head.value; }\n        boolean isEmpty() { return head == null; }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Stack s = new Stack();\n        for (int x : new int[]{10, 20, 30}) s.push(x);\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n        for (Node n = s.head; n != null; n = n.next) sb.append(n.value).append(' ');\n        System.out.println(sb.toString().trim());\n        System.out.println(\"size: \" + s.size);\n        System.out.println(\"popped: \" + s.pop());\n        System.out.println(\"peek: \" + s.peek());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor(value) { this.value = value; this.next = null; }\n}\n\nclass Stack {\n    constructor() { this.head = null; this.size = 0; }\n    push(value) {\n        const node = new Node(value);\n        node.next = this.head;           // new node points at the old top\n        this.head = node;\n        this.size++;\n    }\n    pop() {\n        if (!this.head) throw new Error(\"pop from empty stack\");\n        const node = this.head;\n        this.head = node.next;\n        this.size--;\n        return node.value;\n    }\n    peek() { return this.head ? this.head.value : null; }\n    isEmpty() { return this.head === null; }\n}\n\nconst s = new Stack();\nfor (const x of [10, 20, 30]) s.push(x);\nconst parts = [];\nfor (let n = s.head; n; n = n.next) parts.push(n.value);\nconsole.log(parts.join(\" \"));\nconsole.log(\"size:\", s.size);\nconsole.log(\"popped:\", s.pop());\nconsole.log(\"peek:\", s.peek());</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>30 20 10\nsize: 3\npopped: 30\npeek: 20</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(1) per operation</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n) for n elements</span></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-apps\"></i>Where stacks are used</h3>\n<div class=\"grid grid--3\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-3\"></i>Function calls</h3><p>The call stack stores one frame per active call; returning pops it. Deep recursion overflows it.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i>Balanced brackets</h3><p>Push every opener, pop on every closer and check that it matches — see below.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-back-up\"></i>Undo / redo, back / forward</h3><p>Each action is pushed; undo pops it onto a second stack for redo.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-math-function\"></i>Expression evaluation</h3><p>Infix → postfix conversion and postfix evaluation both run on a stack.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-left-right\"></i>Reversing</h3><p>Push everything, then pop everything — the order reverses.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-bar\"></i>Monotonic stack problems</h3><p>Next greater element, stock span, histogram area (DSA Advanced → Stack).</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i>Problem: check for balanced parentheses</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>s = \"([])\"</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>Yes</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>s = \"([)]\"</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>No</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>s = \"((\"</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>No</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Scan left to right. Push every opening bracket. On a closing bracket the stack must be non-empty and its top must be the matching opener — pop it. At the end the stack must be empty. Any violation means the string is unbalanced.</p></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Opening bracket</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Push it onto the stack.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Closing bracket</div><div class=\"step-desc\">If the stack is empty → unbalanced. Pop the top; if it is not the matching opener → unbalanced.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">End of string</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Balanced only if the stack is empty (every opener was closed).</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def is_balanced(s):\n    match = {')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{'}\n    stack = []\n    for ch in s:\n        if ch in '([{':\n            stack.append(ch)\n        elif ch in match:\n            if not stack or stack.pop() != match[ch]:\n                return False\n    return not stack\n\nfor s in [\"([])\", \"([)]\", \"((\", \"{[()]}\"]:\n    print(s, \"→\", \"Yes\" if is_balanced(s) else \"No\")</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stack&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nbool isBalanced(const string&amp; s) {\n    stack&lt;char&gt; st;\n    for (char ch : s) {\n        if (ch == '(' || ch == '[' || ch == '{') {\n            st.push(ch);\n        } else {\n            if (st.empty()) return false;\n            char open = st.top(); st.pop();\n            if ((ch == ')' &amp;&amp; open != '(') || (ch == ']' &amp;&amp; open != '[') || (ch == '}' &amp;&amp; open != '{'))\n                return false;\n        }\n    }\n    return st.empty();\n}\n\nint main() {\n    for (string s : {\"([])\", \"([)]\", \"((\", \"{[()]}\"})\n        cout &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; \" → \" &lt;&lt; (isBalanced(s) ? \"Yes\" : \"No\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static boolean isBalanced(String s) {\n        Deque&lt;Character&gt; stack = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();\n        for (char ch : s.toCharArray()) {\n            if (ch == '(' || ch == '[' || ch == '{') {\n                stack.push(ch);\n            } else {\n                if (stack.isEmpty()) return false;\n                char open = stack.pop();\n                if ((ch == ')' &amp;&amp; open != '(') || (ch == ']' &amp;&amp; open != '[') || (ch == '}' &amp;&amp; open != '{'))\n                    return false;\n            }\n        }\n        return stack.isEmpty();\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (String s : new String[]{\"([])\", \"([)]\", \"((\", \"{[()]}\"})\n            System.out.println(s + \" → \" + (isBalanced(s) ? \"Yes\" : \"No\"));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function isBalanced(s) {\n    const match = { ')': '(', ']': '[', '}': '{' };\n    const stack = [];\n    for (const ch of s) {\n        if ('([{'.includes(ch)) {\n            stack.push(ch);\n        } else if (ch in match) {\n            if (!stack.length || stack.pop() !== match[ch]) return false;\n        }\n    }\n    return stack.length === 0;\n}\n\nfor (const s of [\"([])\", \"([)]\", \"((\", \"{[()]}\"])\n    console.log(s, \"→\", isBalanced(s) ? \"Yes\" : \"No\");</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>([]) → Yes\n([)] → No\n(( → No\n{[()]} → Yes</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Forgetting the final <code>stack is empty</code> check accepts <code>\"((\"</code>; forgetting the <code>empty before pop</code> check crashes on <code>\")\"</code>. Both are classic interview slips.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview Q&amp;A</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Array or linked list for a stack — which is better?</summary><div><p>Both give O(1) push/pop. An array (dynamic) is cache-friendly and usually faster; occasionally a push triggers an O(n) resize, but the amortised cost stays O(1). A linked list never resizes and has no capacity limit, at the price of one pointer per element and scattered memory.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is stack overflow in the context of recursion?</summary><div><p>Every call pushes a frame on the call stack. Recursion that goes too deep (no base case, or a very large input) exhausts the stack's fixed size — Python raises <code>RecursionError</code> at about 1000 frames; C++ and Java crash with a stack overflow. Convert to iteration or use an explicit stack when depth can be large.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How would you implement a queue using two stacks?</summary><div><p>Push into stack <code>in</code>. To dequeue, if <code>out</code> is empty, pop everything from <code>in</code> and push onto <code>out</code> (this reverses the order), then pop from <code>out</code>. Each element moves at most twice, so operations are amortised O(1). See the Queue topic.</p></div></details></section>"}